course: dictionary
lesson: 15866
title: Ungovernable: beyond control
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ungovernable is built from three parts. Un- means not, govern means to rule or control, and -able means can be. So ungovernable means cannot be ruled or controlled. «The wild horse was ungovernable and would not let anyone ride it.» The clue is that nobody could ride it, so the horse was beyond control.
explain: Ungovernable can describe a place, a crowd, or a feeling. «After the war, the country became ungovernable.» Here the clue is war and chaos: the government could not keep order. «Her temper was ungovernable when she lost the game.» Here the clue is that she shouted and stamped, so her anger was too strong to control.
explain: Always check the context. Ask what would fit in the sentence instead of the hard word. If you swap in "impossible to control" and the sentence still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
fact: Ungovernable means impossible to control or rule.
fact: Un- means not, govern means to rule, and -able means can be, so ungovernable means cannot be ruled.
fact: «Ungovernable» can describe a country, a crowd, a class, or feelings like anger.
fact: «The crowd became ungovernable» means the people could no longer be kept in order.
fact: A good check is to swap in "impossible to control" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The wild horse was ungovernable and would not let anyone ride it.» What does ungovernable mean here? | *Impossible to control | Fast and beautiful | Scared of the dark | Very tired and sleepy
exercise: «After the war, the country became ungovernable.» Why did the country become ungovernable? | The war ended with a big party | The roads were closed for repairs | The people were too happy to work | *The government could not keep order
exercise: Ella says her little brother's temper is ungovernable when he is cross. What does she mean? | *His anger is too strong to control | He loves playing football | He cannot count to ten | He always stays calm and quiet
test: Ungovernable has the prefix un-. What does un- mean here? | Again | Under | *Not | Very
test: «The thunderstorm was so ungovernable that the sailors could not steer the ship.» What does the sentence tell us? | The sailors were asleep | *The storm was beyond control | The storm had ended | The ship was calm and easy to steer
exam: «Anger can feel ungovernable, but a slow, calm breath can help you settle.» What helps when anger feels ungovernable? | Ignoring every feeling you have | Shouting louder | *A slow, calm breath | Running away from home for ever
